# Cloudflare Pages redirects — https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/
# Format:  <source>  <destination>  <status>
# First match wins, so specific rules go above general ones.

# ─── Canonical host ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both www and apex served 200 before this deploy, which is duplicate content.
# The whole site canonicalises to the apex, so www redirects to it.
#
# IMPORTANT: on Cloudflare the authoritative place for a host-level redirect is a
# Redirect Rule in the dashboard, because Pages applies _redirects only after the
# request has already reached this project. This line is a mirror so the intent
# travels with the repo. Create the dashboard rule too:
#     If   hostname equals www.getcerti.com
#     Then dynamic redirect to concat("https://getcerti.com", http.request.uri.path)
#     Status 301, preserve query string
https://www.getcerti.com/*   https://getcerti.com/:splat   301

# ─── Legal documents ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The previous site served these as PDFs via 200-rewrites. They are now proper
# HTML pages, so the old paths redirect to them and there is exactly one live
# version of each document rather than two that can drift apart.
/PrivacyPolicy    /privacy-policy   301
/privacypolicy    /privacy-policy   301
/TaC              /terms            301
/tac              /terms            301

# ─── Retired locale paths ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The old site had /en/ and /tr/. The new site is single-locale English at the root.
/en/*   /   301
/tr/*   /   301

# ─── Retired blog ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All 14 of these were "Coming Soon" stubs of ~120 words, so no real content is
# lost, but they were live and indexed and thin pages are better redirected than
# left to 404. The slugs are a ready-made content plan: when the real blog ships,
# delete the matching line and publish at the same slug so the redirect becomes
# the article. Keep /blog/ itself listed rather than using /blog/* — a wildcard
# would pre-break the new blog the moment it launches.
/blog/                                            /   301
/blog/ai-native-vs-legacy-qms/                    /   301
/blog/best-qms-software-small-manufacturers/      /   301
/blog/brcgs-compliance-packaging-companies/       /   301
/blog/brcgs-criteria-that-fail-most-manufacturers/ /  301
/blog/capa-management-software-root-cause-analysis/ / 301
/blog/certi-qms-challenges-food-packaging-manufacturers/ / 301
/blog/iso-9001-2026-food-manufacturers/           /   301
/blog/iso-9001-compliance-checklist-food-manufacturers/ / 301
/blog/non-conformance-report-template/            /   301
/blog/qms-implementation-under-30-days/           /   301
